  {:id :policy-free-energy :defines :F-pi :node :R8 :class :theory-defined :ref :parr2022
   :imports [:Q-o-pi :o]
   :formal "F_pi := sum_k 1/2 ( ln(2 pi v_k(pi)) + (o_k - mu_k(pi))^2 / v_k(pi) )"
   :eq "parr2022 eq. B.9, p. 247 -- the F term of pi = sigma(ln E - F - G). B.9 names the term and does not fix the likelihood; the Gaussian per-channel form is this stack's reduction, so like :precision this row is theory-defined in ROLE and stack-defined in CONTENT."
   :lean nil :lean-status :missing
   :code "policy_free_energy.clj:41-144 (f-pi-for-candidate), 146-151 (f-pi-vector); war_machine.clj:123-155 (f-pi-posterior-preconditions!), 213-336 (f-pi-dark-readback, the per-tick scoring), 384-461 (f-pi-posterior-opts, the join and the coverage rule), 5095-5096 (the call site); policy.clj:147-203 (softmax-weights, the posterior seam), 413-421 (what the decision records)"
   :live-consumer :policy-posterior
   :status :realised-flag-gated
   :note "HORIZON ONE AND RETROSPECTIVE, the reduction to state plainly: B.9's F_pi is the free energy of observations already made under pi, and the WM scores the PREVIOUS tick's per-candidate predictions against THIS tick's observation, joined by action identity (type + target). A current candidate with no matched previous prediction has no F_pi, and the tick then declines to use the term at all rather than impute one -- COMPLETE-OR-OFF. Measured live: F_pi runs -19.716..-18.962 within a tick against a G spread of 125.34, so imputing 0.0 for an uncovered candidate would hand it the posterior outright. Behind FUTON_WM_FPI_POSTERIOR=1, DEFAULT OFF, coupled to FUTON_WM_FPI_DARK=1 and to FUTON_WM_TRACE_POLICY_DETAILS=1 having been on the PREVIOUS tick; the preconditions refuse the flag without them rather than record a tick that did nothing. Scaling is :unscaled, settled by source and not chosen: friston2017 eq. 2.7 multiplies G by gamma alone and leaves F unscaled, and B.9 has no temperature at all."}
  {:id :belief-update :defines :mu-next :node :R3 :class :theory-defined :ref :buckley2017
   :imports [:mu :Pi :eps :alpha]
   :formal "mu <- mu + alpha Pi eps" :eq "buckley2017 eq. 59 (reduction: one gradient step with fixed step size alpha)" :lean "machineBeliefUpdate" :lean-status :closed
   :lean-note "CARRIER DECLARED 2026-09-05 by :F8 leg 1 slice 3 (this row previously read :lean nil :lean-status :missing). mathlib4/DarkTower/WarMachine/MachineBeliefUpdate.lean declares machineBeliefUpdate as the composite the implementation runs and MachineBeliefUpdateWitness.lean proves its reference values against production (runs/F8-belief-update/clojure-readback.txt: delta 0.0 on every scalar and structural case, and the two categorical posteriors match componentwise within 1e-12). Both modules elaborate with 0 errors and 0 warnings; #print axioms on all 49 declarations gives [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound] on 47, [propext, Quot.sound] on one and no axioms on one, with no sorryAx. THE :formal LINE ABOVE IS NOT THE MAP THE MACHINE RUNS, and that is stated as a theorem rather than as prose. There is no real-valued mu that a step alpha Pi eps is added to. The implementation's belief is a per-entity CATEGORICAL posterior over the seven-status set (belief.clj:37-42), and this tick's evidence reaches it as the TEMPERING EXPONENT of a Bayesian filter step, q <- normalise(A(o|.)^kappa(w) x (B q)) with kappa(w) = log2(1+w) (belief.clj:297-346, reached from production through apply-arena-belief-events, war_machine.clj:821-827, whose likelihood mode is :aif unless FUTON_WM_LIKELIHOOD_MODE is set). registryAdditiveFormIsNotGeneral is the counterexample: at kappa = 0 Bayes leaves the prior fixed while a nonzero alpha Pi eps moves it. WHAT alpha Pi eps BECOMES. The channel errors are aggregated into ONE signed driver, the precision-weighted average sum(sign_c x precision_c x error_c) / sum(precision_c) over contributing channels (belief.clj:1122-1197, signs at belief.clj:1003-1021), returned as a TYPED RECORD with two statuses and never a third -- :present carries a driver, :unknown carries a reason and no number, and there is deliberately no :refused. The driver is then annealed and clamped into an event weight and attributed per entity by INCONSISTENCY with the error direction (war_machine.clj:6157-6187): weight_e = (h_e or 1-h_e) x eventWeight x n / sum of those inconsistencies, so the mean attributed weight is the event weight while individual entities move unequally. THREE PROPERTIES OF THAT PATH, PROVED. (1) THE MAGNITUDE SATURATES: base-weight is min(1, |driver|) (war_machine.clj:6158), so a driver of 1 and a driver of 50 produce the SAME event weight (driverMagnitudeSaturates) -- above 1 the size of the surprise is discarded and only its sign survives. (2) THE MULTICHANNEL FLAG CHANGES THE DRIVER'S UNITS. Scaling every channel's precision coherently leaves the multichannel driver unchanged, because the precisions divide out (precisionDividesOutInMultichannel); in the :annotation-health-only path the driver IS that channel's weighted error, so the same scaling multiplies it (precisionScalesSingleChannel). Under *r3d-multichannel?* false the same precision state therefore drives belief harder, and the readback measures both (driver 1/4 either way multichannel, 1 vs 4 single-channel). (3) THE ANNEAL FLOOR IS UNREACHABLE: annealFactor is max(0, 1 - step/3) and reaches 0 only at step 3, while the inner loop exits at (inc step) >= r3-max-steps = 3 (war_machine.clj:6060, 6224), so production only ever sees steps 0, 1, 2 (annealTerminates with productionStepsDoNotReachAnnealTermination). AND THE UPDATE IS NOT SIGN-SYMMETRIC, measured not assumed: a :strengthened and a :foreclosed event of the same weight on a uniform prior move expected health by +0.0812 and -0.0633, summing to +0.0179 (updateIsNotSignSymmetric; the asymmetry is in the observation model's columns, not in the filter). AN :unknown DRIVER APPLIES NO EVENT rather than a zero one (unknownDriverAppliesNoEvent), which is the AC2 ruling's shape carried into Lean. STATED LIMIT OF THE LEAN EXPECTED-HEALTH: MachineBeliefUpdateWitness.expectedHealth is (raw + 1)/2 without the [0,1] clamp production applies (belief.clj:625-638). The clamp is inert on a normalised posterior, which is the only input the filter produces, so the two agree everywhere the readback measures; off that domain they are different functions and the Lean one is not the implementation. NOT A RULING -- nothing is written to :choices, and no claim is made about whether the saturation, the flag-dependent units or the asymmetry should change. NOT CLAIMED: nothing about the R8 errors that feed the driver (a different row, closed by slice 2), nothing about the :legacy and :a-matrix likelihood modes the env hatch can select, and no live tick was run. POINTER DEFECT FOUND AND NOT REPAIRED, the same class slice 2 reported one row over: this row's :code below reads war_machine.clj:4375-4388, 4429-4431, which at futon2 HEAD is inside scan-blocks (the Block-footer commit scanner) and the markdown table renderer, neither of which has anything to do with the belief update. The aggregation is belief.clj:1122-1197, the tick's inner step is war_machine.clj:6147-6190 and the filter is belief.clj:297-346. :code is the field TN 9a gates, so it is named here and left as it stands."
   :lean-at "mathlib4 0e89cc1cb5 (MachineBeliefUpdate.lean, MachineBeliefUpdateWitness.lean; parent d15325c004, the modules as first committed)"
   :code "war_machine.clj:4375-4388, 4429-4431"}
