  :gamma-fixed-point
  {:status :decided
   :statement "TWO questions, and the source settles the first. WITHIN A TICK: pi = sigma(-F - gamma.G) and the beta update are mutually dependent -- gamma needs pi, and the policy posterior that defines pi imports tau = 1/gamma. friston2017 states the scheme outright: 'usually one would iterate the equalities in equation 2.7 until convergence' (friston2017.txt:683-685), with the gradient form in appendix B (:1690-1720) as the robust alternative. That is the formalism's answer, not an option. ACROSS TICKS: eq. 2.7 reads beta_posterior = beta_prior + (pi - pi0).G -- two distinct symbols the text extraction collapses. P(gamma) = Gamma(1, beta_prior) at :339 and Q(gamma) = Gamma(1, beta_posterior) at :357 are the prior/posterior pair, and the precision prediction error at :1711 is eps_gamma = (beta_prior - beta_posterior) + (pi - pi0).G, which would be identically zero in its first term if the two were one symbol. What the paper does NOT say is what beta_prior is at the start of each tick. That silence is the choice point."
   :options {:carried-prior "The previous tick's converged beta_posterior becomes this tick's beta_prior -- precision learned across ticks, the shape futon2.aif.selection-gain already uses (read/coerce at war_machine.clj:4332-4361, written back at :4773-4776). The machine gets more or less decisive as its policy evidence accumulates. Keeps the PERSISTENCE SHAPE of today's default path, and nothing else."
             :fixed-prior "beta_prior resets to a declared beta_0 every tick. Each tick's decisiveness is set only by this tick's G and pi; nothing about precision is remembered. Keeps the WITHIN-TICK FRESHNESS of FUTON_WM_TAU_MODE=spread, which is not the default. Simpler, and the arm against which carrying is measurable."}
   :control "TODAY'S DEFAULT LIVE PATH IS NEITHER ARM, and that is what dissolves the argument about which one it resembles (claude-1, 2026-09-01). On the default :selection-gain-only mode, tau comes from a CARRIED state that never updates live -- fold-realized-outcome's own docstring says the :realized-outcome field is absent today, sim-only (C2, C454) -- so today is a carried prior that does not move. Each arm therefore resembles today in exactly one aspect and departs in the other: :carried-prior keeps the persistence and adds the movement; :fixed-prior keeps the staticness and drops the persistence. The experiment's control must be the default live path itself, not either arm. A control chosen from the two things being compared is the failure this campaign keeps finding one level down."
   :ruling {:by "joe" :at "2026-09-01" :grounds :ruling :decision :carried-prior
            :statement "beta_prior at the start of each tick is the previous tick converged beta_posterior. beta_0 remains required as the first-tick prior and must be declared and reported, never defaulted silently."
            :implied-by-the-source "An argument from the paper structure, not a sentence in it. friston2017 splits the approximate posterior into states and parameters -- x = (s_tilde, pi, eta) with eta = (a, b, d, beta) (:343) -- so beta is a PARAMETER, in the same tuple as the Dirichlet concentrations. Of that side the paper says the updates 'accumulate coincidences over time, because parameters are time invariant (in contrast to states that change over time)' (:815). States are re-derived per tick; parameters accumulate. :fixed-prior would move a member of eta onto the state side of the paper own division. Joe reached this before the check: 'the theory should say beta is carried, as i think that is implied'."
            :grounds-audit "2026-09-01, after the V5 cross-check against SPM (C463). Joe gave TWO grounds; only one survives, and the entry says which. SURVIVES -- the tick grain: a WM tick is an SPM TIME STEP, not a trial. An SPM trial is an episode whose start re-initialises beliefs from D/d, re-enumerates the policy set, and returns qb to the prior; the WM has no such boundary, carries belief tick to tick, and never re-initialises from a prior, so it is one unending trial, and within a trial SPM carries beta across time steps exactly as ruled (spm_MDP_VB_X.m:433-434, :951-974). CONTRADICTED -- the parameter tuple: the argument that beta sits in eta = (a, b, d, beta) with the parameters that accumulate is not how the reference implementation treats it, since spm_MDP_update carries a, b, c, d and e between trials and NOT beta (spm_MDP_VB_X.m:1444-1448). The ruling stands on the first ground. Tick-grain decided by claude-20 as steward under Joe bar for J rows (worklist J5), not referred back to him."
            :run-result "RUN, 2026-09-01 (V6, C464-beta-carry-experiment.md, futon2 a08b90b + 9b6eac0). Three arms over both recorded fields, beta_0 swept {0.5, 1, 2, 5}, control = today default path. THE ARMS DIFFER: carried and fixed orderings diverge on 14 to 36 transitions in every field/start combination, so the choice is real. THE ARGMAX NEVER MOVES: not once across 54 transitions and four starts, so on these fields the choice changes no selection. THE CARRY DOES NOT DIVERGE: beta stays in 0.28..5.58, nowhere near the floor or the ceiling. AND THE HORIZON IS LONGER THAN OUR EVIDENCE. On 07-04 the four trajectories contract to within 0.001 of each other by t=37 -- the carry forgets its initial condition -- but at t=17 they are still 0.50, 0.87, 1.58 and 2.48 apart. 07-05 has only 17 transitions, so its apparent path dependence is a LENGTH artifact, not a property of that field. A carried beta over a 20-tick run is therefore still dominated by its beta_0, and any run of that length must report beta_0 beside every beta and must not describe the carry as converged. The ruling is not contradicted: carrying and resetting are genuinely different, they just do not reach the top of the ranking on the fields we have."
            :also-to-be-run "Joe's rule of 2026-09-01 applies to his own ruling: :carried-prior against :fixed-prior over a recorded field, with the control being today's default path, reported as numbers beside the ruling rather than instead of it. If the numbers contradict the ruling that is a RESULT he wants, not a J row. Dispatched as worklist V6; beta_0 is a parameter of both arms and is reported."
            :consequence "I1 unblocked and built on :carried-prior. The :fixed-prior arm is retained below as the experiment control arm, not as a live option."}
   :leaning {:by "joe" :at "2026-09-01" :toward :carried-prior
             :note "A LEANING, not a ruling: worklist J4 stays :needs-joe. Joe, 2026-09-01: 'this is a good example of where we have a choice provided to us by the absence of any declaration from the theory... perhaps we are going to want to record the choice point, and develop some experiments looking at the two different ones... My leaning would be towards the second option.' His leaning was given against an earlier framing that offered within-tick iteration and across-tick carry as EXCLUSIVE alternatives (worklist J4 as first written, claude-20). They are not exclusive -- claude-1 caught this on 2026-09-01 and the source confirms it. The leaning survives the reframing: it was toward carrying, and carrying is now the :carried-prior arm of the second question, with the first question answered by the paper rather than by anyone here."}
   :experiment "Not yet run, and it does not need a live run: both arms are computable against a recorded field. Take a trace tick's ranked actions and G; iterate 2.7 to convergence from (a) a fixed beta_0 and (b) the previous tick's converged beta; report for each the converged gamma, the iteration count, whether it converged inside a bound at all, and the RANK CHANGES produced -- all three against the control, today's default live path, not against each other. BETA_0 IS A PARAMETER OF BOTH ARMS, not just the fixed one (claude-1, 2026-09-01): the carry arm has no previous tick at the first tick of any recorded sequence, so it starts from a declared beta_0 too. If beta_0 is not reported the two arms differ by an initial condition nobody chose, and the comparison measures that instead of the thing. A gamma difference that changes no ranking is not a difference worth ruling on. The iteration count and the non-convergence rate are the other half of the result: the WM has no iteration budget today, so 'it converges in k steps on real fields' is itself a finding."
